course: dictionary
lesson: 7746
title: A word from another land
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words travel from one country to another. «They held a palaver, and an indaba, and a punchayet, and a pow-wow.» Four words, from different lands, all mean the same thing: a meeting to talk things over. Even if you have never met punchayet before, the words around it give you a strong clue.
explain: A punchayet, also spelled panchayat, is a village council in India. Its name comes from the Hindi word «panch», which means five. Traditionally, it was a council of five elders who settled disputes and ran local affairs.
explain: Rudyard Kipling grew up in India, and he used Indian words in his stories. In the Just So Stories, punchayet means something different: a meeting or council of animals to decide a matter. So one word can have two meanings, and you must check which one fits the sentence.
fact: Punchayet is an older spelling of panchayat.
fact: Panch means five in Hindi.
fact: A punchayet is a village council that settles disputes and runs local affairs.
fact: In the Just So Stories, Kipling uses punchayet for a meeting of animals.
fact: Palaver, indaba, pow-wow and punchayet all mean a meeting to talk things over.
exercise: «They held a palaver, and an indaba, and a punchayet, and a pow-wow.» What does punchayet mean here? | A type of food | A long walk | A kind of dance | *A meeting to talk things over
exercise: Hindi «panch» means five. What did a traditional punchayet have? | A race of five runners | *A council of five elders | A single judge | A group of ten children
exercise: In Kipling's Just So Stories, what is a punchayet? | A sleepy bird | A jungle song | *A meeting or council of animals | A kind of river
test: Which spelling is the modern one? | pancha | punchayat | *panchayat | panchayet
test: In Hindi, what does panch mean? | Ten | *Five | Three | One
exam: Why do you think the author used four words that all mean the same thing? | To confuse the reader | Because all four words are wrong | *To show that meetings to talk things over happen in many lands | To make the sentence longer for no reason
